Third-party material in scion-installer, and what it builds from

In this repository:

- tools/gpt-plan.py: its GPT readers (the header, the entry array, the protective MBR) are adapted from tools/dualboot.py of
  scion-blazer, by Anders Madsen (TheAndersMadsen), MIT, imported into Scion's organisation with his permission. scion-blazer is
  itself based on dgigantino/ios-tegu. The plan, its refusals and its tests are written fresh. scion-blazer's tools/gpt_plan.py
  states no licence, so it was read for its design only and none of it is copied.
- universal/scion-install.sh and tools/test_scion_install.py: written fresh. Their design (hash the images before any write, write BOOT only
  for a kernel update, never touch RECOVERY, keep a RAM copy of BOOT and put it back on a bad read-back) follows the installer of
  danstronics/ios-s9plus-exynos (an S9+ port based on Scion), as summarised in scion-kernel's docs/star2lte-absorb.md. None of its code
  is used, and its hashes and offsets are its own build's; this one reads its facts from the installer.prop of the user's own build.
- Everything else here is under this repository's LICENSE (MIT).

Fetched or built on the user's machine, never committed (their own licences):

- Scion's kernel and loader (github.com/Scion-Project/scion-kernel, a fork of HakiOS by the HakiOS authors; see its NOTICE).
- theos and its SDKs, Procursus and Sileo packages, and the rest build-installer.sh fetches: each under its own licence.
- The iOS 16.5 IPSW, which the user supplies: Apple's, never in this repository and never downloaded by it. No Apple software,
  vendor firmware or keys are in this repository.
